{"author":{"id":"2de4467f26c42fb223f4d3ddd5611e7c0513d3daae4c1dc315de039036bc997d"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778952292,"nonce":"hPoQV+8LN+fZ2RHl+4idoecPjq8=","title":"Harden raw HTML rendering paths","message":"## Problem\nSeveral rendering paths build HTML strings and inject them with `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` without a central sanitizer or structured markdown rendering path.\n\nExamples found during audit:\n- `renderInlineMarkdown()` uses regex replacement and does not escape raw HTML before inserting links/code/emphasis.\n- Project descriptions are rendered through `remark-html` with `sanitize: false`.\n- Search snippets are generated as HTML and later highlighted/injected in the client.\n- Footnotes and metadata fields also use inline HTML injection.\n\n## Context\nMost inputs are author-controlled content, so this is lower risk than user-submitted HTML. The issue is still important because private writing content is pulled in during CI from another repo, and a malformed title/excerpt/footnote can become executable markup on the public site.\n\n## Potential Solutions\n- Replace regex-based inline markdown HTML with a structured parser plus sanitizer, or return React nodes instead of strings.\n- Define a single allowed-inline-markup policy for titles, excerpts, metadata, footnotes, and search snippets.\n- Keep richer MDX rendering for trusted full post bodies, but make short metadata fields intentionally narrow.\n- Add fixture tests for escaping `\u003cscript\u003e`, event-handler attributes, and `javascript:` links in frontmatter/excerpts/footnotes.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}